{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and a key figure in the Crusader states during the twelfth century. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and inherited the county at a young age after his father was assassinated. His early rule was marked by a regency led by his mother and later by the King of Jerusalem because he was still a minor. Raymond III spent much of his youth in captivity after being taken prisoner by Nur ad-Din Zengi in 1164. His imprisonment lasted nearly ten years and significantly shaped his political outlook and military caution. During this time Tripoli was governed by regents and his mother until his release in 1173.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nUpon his return Raymond III became a dominant force in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was appointed regent of Jerusalem in 1174 after the death of King Baldwin III and served in that role during the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was known for his diplomacy and his desire to avoid unnecessary war with Muslim powers. He often clashed with the militant Knights Templar and the Hospitallers who favored aggressive campaigns. His most notable political maneuver was his alliance with Saladin which he pursued to ensure stability in the region. This alliance drew criticism from many of his fellow crusaders who viewed it as cowardly or treasonous. Raymond also married Eschiva of Bures to strengthen his ties to the royal family and solidify his claim to influence.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who opposed the rash decision to march against Saladin at Tiberias. He warned that the crusader army would be cut off from water and surrounded but his advice was ignored. After the devastating defeat at Hattin Raymond was one of the few high ranking nobles to escape. He retreated to Tripoli and held the city against Saladin’s forces. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem and before the arrival of the Third Crusade. His death marked the end of a long line of capable and cautious rulers in Tripoli. His legacy is that of a pragmatic leader who sought peace in a time of relentless conflict. His life reflects the complex tensions between diplomacy and military zeal in the Crusader states.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.9740936160087585}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.194758028257638e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -7.271740287251305e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "l